# The folder add-owner handler interpolated the request body's owner into a
# raw sqlx query through format! (GHSA-34m2-qrpf-6v7q): the owner value lands
# inside a jsonb_set path argument, '("{\"{owner}\"}")', so an owner carrying
# SQL metacharacters executes as part of the UPDATE statement. The advisory's
# chain reads the JWT signing secret out of the database through it, so the
# recorded CWE-89 matches the mechanism.
#
# The fixed revision adds a charset validation helper (alphanumeric, underscore, hyphen,
# slash) called before the interpolation in both the add-owner and
# remove-owner handlers. The interpolation itself is unchanged at <fix>: the
# patched tree still builds the same query string from format!, and the
# vulnerable form is present at <fix>^ with no validation call. So the two
# revisions differ only by the presence of validate_owner(&owner)? -- the
# discriminator is the added control, not any change in the sink shape.
#
# The taint path from the body to the query is dead at two independent Rust
# extraction constructs, each isolated below at finding level:
#
# 1. A tuple-struct-pattern parameter -- Json(Owner { owner, .. }): Json<Owner>
#    -- extracts no parameter at all. params() and paramTypes() (rust.go)
#    take patName(field(ch, "pattern")), which returns "" for a
#    tuple_struct_pattern, so the handler's Json and Path parameters leave no
#    Param node and no local binding for the destructured owner. The same
#    family as the JavaScript entry for rank 1803 (object-pattern parameters
#    lower to an empty parameter list): the body's owner is a free Name with
#    no incoming edge.
#
# 2. An implicit format capture -- format!("... {owner} ...") -- keeps the
#    identifier inside the template string literal. The macro's token tree
#    holds one string-literal child, so the Format node's parts carry the
#    template Const and no expression for the captured variable; taint from
#    the variable cannot reach the format result. An explicit positional
#    capture, format!("{}", owner), does produce a Name part and reports.
#
# Both halves of the discrimination question are measured and rejected:
# - A DynamicSqlQuery binding on sqlx::query over a format-built string (the
#   concept exists, VYQL-INJ-203, Python-only today) fires on the vulnerable
#   and the patched form alike, because the patched form still formats the
#   query; it cannot score the fixed revision below the parent.
# - A negation on the validate_owner call is the fixing commit's own
#   vocabulary (the helper is new in the fixed revision); renamed, the suppression
#   disappears. The control itself is a same-file free function, which does
#   resolve, but no cross-procedural fact attributes its body to the caller
#   (the Rust control-attribution gap, rank 1364 family).
#
# Reach: windmill's backend has 859 extractor-typed parameters across 57
# files, and 446 of its 2184 format! calls use implicit named captures;
# corpus-wide 3402 of 8667 format! calls across the clones do. So both
# constructs are ordinary Rust, not windmill-specific.
#
# Flip conditions: when implicit format captures lower as value parts, case
# 4 flips to expect; when a pattern parameter lowers to a parameter plus
# per-leaf bindings, case 6 flips to expect. Cases 1 and 2 flip only when
# both of those land plus a source binding for axum extractor parameters,
# and case 2 stays reject only once a neutralising control is attributable
# across the sibling call (otherwise both revisions report).

test "rank1951: the folder add-owner handler interpolates the body's owner into a jsonb_set path query"
  lang rust
  file src/folders.rs
  reject VYQL-INJ-001
  code
  ```
  use axum::Json;
  use serde::Deserialize;

  #[derive(Deserialize)]
  pub struct Owner {
      pub owner: String,
      pub edit: bool,
  }

  async fn add_owner(
      Path((w_id, name)): Path<(String, String)>,
      Json(Owner { owner, .. }): Json<Owner>,
  ) -> Result<String> {
      sqlx::query(&format!(
          "UPDATE folder SET extra_perms = jsonb_set(extra_perms, '{{\"{owner}\"}}', to_jsonb($1), \
           true) WHERE name = $2 AND workspace_id = $3 RETURNING extra_perms"
      ))
      .bind(true)
      .fetch_optional(&mut *tx)
      .await?;

      Ok(format!("Added {} to folder {}", owner, name))
  }
  ```

test "rank1951: the patched handler validates the owner charset before the same interpolation"
  lang rust
  file src/folders.rs
  reject VYQL-INJ-001
  code
  ```
  use axum::Json;
  use serde::Deserialize;

  #[derive(Deserialize)]
  pub struct Owner {
      pub owner: String,
      pub edit: bool,
  }

  fn validate_owner(owner: &str) -> Result<()> {
      if !owner
          .chars()
          .all(|c| c.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || c == '_' || c == '/' || c == '-')
      {
          return Err(Error::BadRequest(
              "Invalid owner: must contain only alphanumeric characters, underscores, hyphens, or slashes".to_string(),
          ));
      }
      Ok(())
  }

  async fn add_owner(
      Path((w_id, name)): Path<(String, String)>,
      Json(Owner { owner, .. }): Json<Owner>,
  ) -> Result<String> {
      validate_owner(&owner)?;
      sqlx::query(&format!(
          "UPDATE folder SET extra_perms = jsonb_set(extra_perms, '{{\"{owner}\"}}', to_jsonb($1), \
           true) WHERE name = $2 AND workspace_id = $3 RETURNING extra_perms"
      ))
      .bind(true)
      .fetch_optional(&mut *tx)
      .await?;

      Ok(format!("Added {} to folder {}", owner, name))
  }
  ```

test "rank1951: an explicit positional capture carries the source into the query"
  lang rust
  file src/explicit.rs
  expect VYQL-INJ-001
  code
  ```
  fn sync_owners() {
      let owner = std::env::var("OWNER").unwrap();
      let q = format!("UPDATE folder SET owners = '{}' WHERE workspace_id = $1", owner);
      sqlx::query(&q).fetch_optional();
  }
  ```

